## INTRODUCTION
Carbon dioxide capture and sequestration at power stations and chemical manufacturing plants is anticipated to play an important role in efforts to mitigate greenhouse gas emissions.1 Aqueous amine solutions are already used to capture \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) at some locations, including the recently retrofitted Petra Nova power station.2 More energy efficient capture materials could accelerate the adoption of \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) capture as a mitigation strategy, and amine-functionalized adsorbents have emerged as promising next-generation materials that benefit from the highly selective chemistry of amines with \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) , similar to traditional amine solvents.3 These materials may offer improvements over amine solutions including improved stability, reduced volatility, and lower regeneration temperatures.
Amine-appended metal–organic frameworks with the formula (diamine) \(_{2}\mathrm{M}_{2}\) (dobpdc)—subsequently referred to here as diamine– \(\mathbf{M}_{2}\) (dobpdc)—in particular have shown exceptional promise for \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) capture4–13 and are obtained by functionalizing the coordinatively-unsaturated metal sites in the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}\) (dobpdc) framework \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},\) Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Zn; dobpc4 \(\mathbf{\Psi}^{+}=4,4^{\prime}\) -dioxidobiphenyl\(3,3^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylate) with diamines (Figure 1a,b).4,5,14–19 Rather than displaying traditional Langmuir-type adsorption, many diamine– \(\mathbf{M}_{2}\) (dobpdc) materials exhibit step-shaped isotherms, corresponding to sudden adsorption of \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) upon exposure to a given thresh
old pressure. This behavior arises as a result of an unprecedented cooperative adsorption mechanism whereby \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) inserts into each metal–amine bond to form ion-paired ammonium carbamate chains that propagate along the framework channels (down the crystallographic \(c\) axis, see Figure 1c).5,14,20 Importantly, these materials exhibit high working capacities for \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) removal, and moreover relatively small changes in pressure and temperature can be used to trigger adsorption or desorption. Thus, these materials may afford substantial energy and cost savings when used in processes involving temperature or temperature-pressure swings.21–23
Recent single-crystal X-ray diffraction characterization of a series of diamine–\(\cdot\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\) (dobpdc) compounds revealed that the formation of ammonium carbamate chains occurs for a number of different diamines with diverse structures,14 corroborating the results of other studies using powder X-ray diffraction.6,19 The cooperative adsorption mechanism has been further validated at the atomic level using van der Waals-corrected density functional theory (vdW-corrected DFT) calculations,5,20,24,25 which are able to predict binding enthalpies within a few \(\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) of experiment. These binding enthalpies, in combination with a statistical mechanical model, have been used to explain the step-shaped isotherm in terms of an abrupt increase in the mean chain length at a critical \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) pressure.26 Importantly, the adsorption thermodynamics—and therefore the step pressure at a given temperature—can be tuned by varying the met
al\(^{5,15,20,27}\) or the diamine,\(^{14-16,218}\) allowing for the targeted design of materials for specific separations involving \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). While single-crystal X-ray diffraction studies have been applied with great success to diamine-appended \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) variants,\(^{14,15,19}\) the lack of large single crystals for the most technologically relevant magnesi-
We recently discovered that the material \((\mathrm{dmpn})_{2}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) \((\mathrm{dmpn}=2,2\)-dimethyl-1,3-diaminopropane) possesses an adsorption step in the optimum range for capture of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from coal flue gas, enabling a working capacity of \(2.42\ \mathrm{mmol/g}\) (9.1 wt \(\%\) ) with a modest \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) temperature swing for adsorbent regeneration using pure \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\).\(^{15}\) This material is also stable for at least 1000 adsorption–desorption cycles under humid conditions. Interestingly, gas adsorption, NMR, and X-ray diffraction measurements indicated that this material chemisorbs \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) by a mechanism that does not involve purely ammonium carbamate chain formation (Figure 1c). Indeed, NMR measurements showed that \((\mathrm{dmpn})_{2}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) exhibits a mixed chemisorption mechanism that involves the formation of both ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid species.\(^{16}\) Crystallographic and NMR studies of the isostructural \((\mathrm{dmpn})_{2}\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) analogue revealed that, rather than ammonium carbamate chains, carbamic acid pairs form at \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas pressures close to 1 bar,\(^{15}\) a species that was first predicted computationally in this class of materials (Figure 1d).\(^{20,25,29}\) At pressures below 1 bar, a mixed mechanism was again observed, with an unspecified arrangement of ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid groups.\(^{15}\)
Here, we make use of the well-defined and readily modified structures of the diamine– \(\cdot\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) materials class to perform a
comprehensive study of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) chemisorption using a combination of multinuclear NMR spectroscopy experiments and vdW– corrected DFT calculations. Many different diamine-appended variants can be synthesized and analyzed using this highly ordered system, making these materials invaluable for fundamental studies of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) chemisorption. Furthermore, the techniques and spectroscopic signatures identified here may allow diamine– \(\cdot\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) frameworks to serve as a model system for the broader class of amine-functionalized solids, such as porous silicas, in which the host structure and spatial distribution of amines are often more challenging to control and characterize.\(^{30-33}\) Specifically, we have endeavored to: (i) develop a combined NMR and DFT approach to distinguish between ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid formation, (ii) determine ranges of NMR parameters for these mechanisms so that researchers may more readily make structural assignments, (iii) understand the mixed chemisorption mechanism and unique \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption properties of \((\mathrm{dmpn})_{2}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\), and (iv) to investigate the effect of water on \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) chemisorption mechanisms.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of Ammonium Carbamate Chains. The material \(\mathbf{e}{-}2{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}\) (dobpdc) \(\left(\mathbf{e}{-}2\ =\ N\right)\) -ethylethylenediamine) was recently proposed to form ammonium carbamate chains exclusively upon adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Figure 1c),14 based on its stepshaped adsorption isotherms that saturate at a loading of one \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecule per diamine, as well as infrared spectra of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) - dosed material. These results were also consistent with data collected for the isostructural framework \(\mathbf{e}{-}2{-}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}\) (dobpdc), for which the formation of ammonium carbamate chains upon exposure to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was also confirmed via in situ single-crystal X-ray diffraction experiments. We thus chose to first investigate \(\mathbf{e}{-}2-\) \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) in detail with NMR spectroscopy, as a benchmark material to confirm and explore the formation of ammonium carbamate chains. Studies of gas-dosed samples were enabled by a home-built apparatus (Figure 2) that allows rotors (sample holders) for magic angle spinning (MAS) NMR experiments to be sealed at different pressures of \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas \((99\%\ ^{13}\mathrm{C})\) .15 Following evacuation, activated samples were dosed with \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at a pressure of \({\sim}1\) bar that is above the adsorption step for this material at \(25~^{\circ}\!C\) (see Figure S1). The samples were then allowed to equilibrate before they were capped inside the manifold using a move
able plunger. This dosing system is similar to those previously reported for studies of zeolites34 and amine-functionalized silicas.35
The \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR spectra of \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) -dosed \(\mathbf{e}{-}2{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}\) (dobpdc) exhibit resonances assignable to both chemisorbed and physisorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at 162.1 and \(124.7\,\mathrm{ppm}\) , respectively (Figure 3a). Experiments on two independent samples (see Supporting Information, Figure S2), as well as experiments by another research group,36 have shown that the \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) chemical shift for chemisorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is reproducible within \({\sim}0.1\ \mathrm{ppm}\) . The chemical shift of physisorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is within \(0.4\;\mathrm{ppm}\) of the values observed for physisorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in various diamine-appended \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) materials (Figure S3), although the resonance is shifted by \(-3\ \mathrm{ppm}\) relative to that of free gas-phase \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) ( \(127.7\ \mathrm{ppm}\) at 1 bar).28 This chemical shift difference may arise in part due to aromatic ring currents in the dobpcd4– linkers.37,38 Quantitative \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR indicates that at 1 bar only a small percentage \((10{-}15\%)\) of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is physisorbed, and thus adsorption primarily occurs via chemisorption
at this pressure. As expected, cross-polarization from \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow{}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) revealed only the resonance from chemisorbed \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) as the strong \(^{1}\mathrm{H}^{-13}\mathrm{C}\) dipole–dipole couplings necessary for efficient cross-polarization are absent for the physisorbed gas.
Additional NMR experiments enabled the assignment of the chemisorbed species as ammonium carbamate, consistent with the conclusions of earlier work.14 In particular, a two dimensional \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow{}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) HETCOR spectrum with a short contact time \((100\,\upmu\mathrm{s})\) allowed detection of only those hydrogen atoms near the \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) nucleus of chemisorbed \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Figure 3b). This spectrum shows a dominant \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) correlation at \(4.3\;\mathrm{ppm},\) as well as a minor correlation at \(13.2\,\mathrm{ppm}\) . We have assigned the former to the \(\mathrm{N-H}\) of the carbamate group (i.e., \(\mathrm{NHRCOO^{-}}\) ), for which a dominant correlation is anticipated because this hydrogen is the closest to the
carbamate \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) carbon \((^{13}\mathrm{C},...^{1}\mathrm{H}=2.05\,\mathrm{\AA},\) from the DFT structure). The \(\mathrm{N-H}\) correlation confirms reaction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with the primary amine group, rather than the secondary amine. Reaction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with the secondary amine would form a tertiary nitrogen with no attached hydrogens, and so the \(\mathrm{N-H}\) correlation would be absent. The minor correlation at \(13.2\;\mathrm{ppm}\) is attributed to an ammonium hydrogen \((^{13}\mathrm{C}...^{1}\mathrm{H}=2.43\,\mathrm{\AA}\) from DFT) and supports the formation of ammonium carbamate chains, in which carbamate and ammonium groups are ion-paired and strongly hydrogen bonded. We note that ammonium carbamate chain formation is necessary here to achieve the observed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity of one \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) per diamine (see Figure S1), which excludes the formation of other ammonium carbamate species
Characterization of Carbamic Acid Pairs. The material dmnp– \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) was selected as a model system for the detailed study of carbamic acid pairs in this class of MOFs. Unlike dmnp– \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) , which adsorbs \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) by a mixed chemisorption mechanism (see below), \(\mathrm{dmnp-Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) forms carbamic acid pairs when dosed at \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) pressures close to 1 bar, as confirmed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction and \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) solid-state NMR spectroscopy.15 Although molecular carbamic acids have also been characterized crystallographically,43 this result represents the only crystallographic characterization to date of a carbamic acid pair structure in an amino-functionalized adsorbent, and so serves as a valuable model system for further study by NMR.
Figure 4a shows the \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\,\rightarrow\,^{13}\mathrm{C}\) cross-polarization spectrum of \(\mathrm{dmnp-Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) dosed with 1015 mbar of \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . The spectrum exhibits a major resonance at \(161.2\ \mathrm{ppm}\) , similar to that observed in our previous work,15 which we assign to carbamic acid pairs. This chemical shift is similar to those previously assigned to carbamic acids in the literature.35,40,41,44–46 The weak resonance at \({\sim}164\,\mathrm{ppm}\) likely arises from a minor amount of ammonium carbamate (see Figure S5). Importantly, a two dimensional \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow{}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) HETCOR experiment (short contact time) revealed two major correlations at \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) chemical shifts of 12.2 and \(5.7\,\mathrm{ppm}\) (Figure 4b), assigned to hydrogens of the carbamic acid (NHCOOH and NHCOOH, respectively). Importantly, this result contrasts to that for the ammonium carbamate chains, where one major and one minor correlation are observed (Figure
