We performed similar NMR measurements on a series of diamine-appended \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) materials in which the diamine was varied (Table 1). All of the materials exhibit step-shaped \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms,14,15,19 and were studied under \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) pressures much greater than the step pressure. Under these conditions, the materials listed in Table 1 all display NMR spectra consistent with the formation of ammonium carbamate chains (Figures S7-S9), supporting the hypothesis that this mechanism dominates for a wide range of diamines.14,15,19 The key evidence for ammonium carbamate chain formation is the observation of \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) resonances that correlate strongly with a carbamate \(\mathrm{N-H}\) group and weakly with an ammonium group in two-dimensional experiments. The observation of ammonium resonances in the \(^{15}\mathrm{N}\) NMR spectra provides additional support for ammonium carbamate chain formation. Additionally, the measured chemical shifts generally show good agreement with DFT-calculated values for ammonium carbamate chain structures (see below). For additional discussion on \(\mathrm{m-2-Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) and \(\mathrm{i-2-Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\), see Figures S7 and S10, respectively.
The large \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) chemical shifts of the ammonium resonances (15.1– \(7.2\;\mathrm{ppm}\)) are consistent with the formation of hydrogen-bonded ion pairs.14 The secondary and tertiary ammonium \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) chemical shifts \((15.1{-}12.3\;\mathrm{ppm})\) are well predicted by our \(0\,\mathrm{K}\) DFT calculations, indicating that the alkyl ammonium groups do not rotate significantly on a millisecond timescale, likely due to steric restrictions within the pores of the material. In contrast, for primary ammonium groups \(\left(\mathrm{RNH}_{3}^{+}\right)\), smaller chemical shifts of \(7{-}8\;\mathrm{ppm}\) are observed, together with somewhat stronger resonances. Averaging of the DFT-calculated values for the three different \(_\mathrm{H}\) at-
oms was required to obtain reasonable agreement between calculation and experiment, suggesting that these primary ammonium groups do rotate on the NMR timescale. An average of calculated \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) chemical shifts for \(\mathrm{RNH}_{3}^{+}\)groups was also shown to result in agreement between experiment and theory in previous studies of glycine in the solid state, where room temperature rotation of \(\mathrm{RNH}_{3}^{+}\)groups is known.48,49 In the \(\upalpha\) and \(\upgamma\) polymorphs of glycine, the \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) chemical shifts for the \(\mathrm{RNH}_{3}^{+}\)group are 8.4 and \(8.8\;\mathrm{ppm}\), respectively,48 similar to the \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) chemical shifts measured here for the primary ammonium cations.
