Overall our results indicate that the ammonium carbamate chain formation dominates for diamine–\( \mathrm{Mg}_{2} \) (dobpdc) materials with diverse diamine structures, in agreement with previous work. \( ^{5,14,15,17,18} \) A range of \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shifts from 161.6–163.8 ppm is observed for ammonium carbamate chains in these materials (Table 1, Figure 6), and the observed \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shift of 161.2 ppm for carbamic acid pairs falls just outside of this range (Table 2). Factors such as the number of alkyl groups on the nitrogen atom and the presence of backbone alkyl groups can affect the \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shifts for ammonium carbamate chains, while stereochemistry can also play a role for diastereomeric compounds—\( \mathrm{M}_{2} \) (dobpdc) is chiral and forms as a racemate under typical synthetic conditions. \( ^{19} \)
The \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shifts for chemisorbed species in diamine- appended \( \mathrm{M}_{2} \) (dobpdc) materials, as well as other amine-functionalized adsorbents from the literature \( ^{35,40,41,44,45} \) are shown in Figure 6. The new data for diamine–\( \mathrm{M}_{2} \) (dobpdc) materials extends the known ranges of \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shifts for ammonium carbamates and carbamic acids. Because the shift ranges for ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid species nearly overlap (Figure 6), the use of \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) NMR spectroscopy alone should be
treated with significant caution when making judgments regarding chemisorption mechanisms in amine-functionalized materials, especially given the RMSD value of 2.2 ppm between experimental and calculated \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shift values. As a further note of caution, ammonium bicarbonate formation was recently observed following \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption in amine functionalized mesoporous silicas under wet conditions, with corresponding \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shifts (162–163 ppm) falling within range of those observed for the ammonium carbamate species identified here. \( ^{36,37} \) Similar to the \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shifts, the \( ^{15}\mathrm{N} \) chemical shifts measured here also show variation from compound to compound, further illustrating the importance of additional NMR experiments beyond \( 1\mathrm{D} \) \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) and \( ^{15}\mathrm{N} \) spectra to obtain conclusive mechanistic assignments. Indeed, recent work on amine-functionalized silicas has shown that the use of the anisotropy of the \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shift (rather than simply isotropic chemical shifts) can enable identification of neutral versus charged chemisorption products. \( ^{52} \) Overall, these studies demonstrate that \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \), \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \), and \( ^{15}\mathrm{N} \) NMR experiments, combined with DFT calculations, provide far more reliable structural assignments than 1D NMR experiments and calculations on a single nucleus.
