The assignment of the \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) resonance at \(161.0\ \mathrm{ppm}\) to carbamic acid is further supported by data from \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow{}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) HETCOR experiments at a short contact time of \(100~\upmu\mathrm{s}\) (Figure 7d), which show a strong \(^1\mathrm{H}\) correlation assignable to the COOH group ( \(^1\mathrm{H}\) \(11.3\ \mathrm{ppm},\) , correlation A) and a correlation from a NHRCOOH group ( \(^1\mathrm{H}\;3.1\;\mathrm{ppm},\) , correlation B). The \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) resonance at 162.4 ppm shows a strong \(\mathbf{NHCOO^{-}}\) correlation ( \(^1\mathrm{H}\;3.3\;\mathrm{ppm},\) , correlation C), and surprisingly the carbamate carbon also shows a correlation with the carbamic acid COOH group (correlation D), although this correlation is weaker than it is for the \(161.0\,\mathrm{ppm}\,^{13}\mathrm{C}\) resonance. The latter correlation indicates that the hydrogen of the carbamic acid –OH group is near the carbon of the assigned ammonium carbamate (Figure 7e). Interestingly, the ratio of the integrated areas of the two main resonances (as determined by quantitative \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR experiments) was found to be 1:1.0 and 1:0.9 (left resonance:right resonance) at pressures of 1015 and 26 mbar, respectively, suggesting that the two species are present in nearly equal amounts over this pressure range. Notably, a similar mixed chemisorption behavior was also observed for dmpn– \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) at intermediate gas-dosing pressures—i.e., above the adsorption step but below the pressures where conversion to carbamic acid pairs occurs (see Figure S10). At lower gas pres
sures of 15 and 6.4 mbar, two additional minor resonances are clearly seen for dmpn– \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) at \({\sim}164.6\) and \(163.5~\mathrm{ppm}\) (Figure 7b), assigned to ammonium carbamate species that are not interacting with carbamic acids (see Figure S13).
Overall, the NMR data for dmpn– \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) at \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) pressures close to 1 bar point toward a dominant new chemisorption mechanism with a 1:1 ratio of interacting ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid species. Guided by our NMR results, a vdWcorrected DFT survey of structures with a 1:1 ratio of ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid led to the discovery of the structure shown in Figure 8. Here, ammonium carbamate chains and carbamic acid chains propagate along the \(c\) axis, down the onedimensional channels of the framework. Hydrogen bonds are present in the ammonium carbamate chain and also across the ab plane between the carbamic acid hydrogen and the neighboring, unbound oxygen atom of the carbamate, forming an extended
To further explore the observed chemisorption products, we used DFT to calculate the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption energies \(\left(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)\) associated with their formation (Table 4). Recent vdW-corrected DFT calculations have accurately predicted \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in diamine– \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{M}_{2}\) (dobpdc) materials.20,24,26,27 The experimental adsorption enthalpy for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in dmps– \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) was determined to be approximately \(-70\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) at gas loadings ranging from \(0{-}2.8\,\mathrm{\mmol/g},\) and this value decreased to about \(-53\) \(\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) at a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) loading of \(3.2\;\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}\) .15 The mixed chemisorption structure introduced above is calculated to have \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}=-54\) \(\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) (for full \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity, i.e., \(1\;\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecule per diamine, see Table 4), agreeing with the heat of adsorption determined at the highest experimental loading. The calculated value is very slightly larger in magnitude than that predicted for carbamic acid pairs \(\left(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}=-53\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\right)\) , which are not observed experimentally in dmpn– \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc). Additional DFT calculations of a pure ammonium carbamate chain structure yielded \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}~=~-69\) \(\mathrm{kJ/mol},\) which is \(15\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) larger in magnitude than that for the mixed structure. While DFT predicts the pure ammonium carbamate chain structure to be the most enthalpically favored at full \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) loading (i.e., at \({\sim}1\) bar \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) ), our experimental NMR data clearly exclude a pure ammonium carbamate mechanism under these conditions (Figure 7). Kinetic or entropic effects,53 which are not accounted for in the DFT calculations, may partly explain the discrepancy between the calculated adsorption energies and the structures observed by NMR spectroscopy.
At \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) pressures below 1 bar, the NMR results (Figure 7b) reveal a combination of ammonium carbamate chains and the mixed chemisorption structure (carbamic acid–ammonium carbamate adducts). Interestingly, at \(50\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity, DFT calculations predict a possible structure containing \(2/3\) ammonium carbamates and \(1/3\) carbamic acids (Figure S16 with \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}=-73\) \(\mathrm{kJ/mol},\) close to the heat of adsorption determined at the corresponding experimental \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) loading. Note that to obtain equili
brated samples, we dosed freshly activated dmnp– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) for at least \(13\,\mathrm{h}\) . Shorter dosing times (as well as wet conditions, see below) resulted in larger amounts of ammonium carbamate species not interacting with carbamic acid (based on \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR data; see Figure S17), suggesting that ammonium carbamate chains are the kinetic product, while the mixed structure is the
thermodynamic product. Finally, the carbamic acid pairs appear to be the most enthalpically favored chemisorption structure at the maximum \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) loading (Table 4) in dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\) (dobpdc), consistent with the observation of these species at high dosing pressures by NMR and X-ray diffraction.15
The mixed chemisorption mechanism proposed for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) yields interacting ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid chains in the \(a b\) plane (Figure 8). To explore the role of these interactions in driving the mixed product formation, we sought to increase the distance between adsorption sites in the \(a b\) plane by studying \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in dmpn– \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) (dotpdc4- \(=\) \(4,4^{\prime\prime}\) -dioxido- \([1,1^{\prime};4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}\) -terphenyl]- \(^{3,3^{\prime\prime}}\) -dicarboxylate). The key structural difference between \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpc})\) and \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) is the larger minimum M···M distance across the ab plane—approximately \(15\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) compared to \({\sim}10\,\mathrm{\AA}\) in \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) .54 Importantly, the distance between metal centers along the \(c\) axis is anticipated to be very similar in both frameworks.16
The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption profile of dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) is broad compared to that of dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc), which suggests a primarily non-cooperative adsorption mechanism (Figure 9a). Indeed, NMR characterization of dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) (Figure \(9\mathbf{b},\mathbf{c}\)) revealed a complex mixture of ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid species, quantified overall in a 1:0.5 ratio, respectively, rather than the 1:1 mixed structure observed for dmpn– \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) . We note that sharp \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm steps
have been observed previously for a range of diamine-appended \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) variants, including \(n\mathrm{Pr}{-}2{-}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) ( \(n\mathrm{Pr}{-}2=N.\) - propylethylenediamine),16 indicating that such materials are capable of cooperative adsorption by ammonium carbamate chain formation. Indeed, NMR measurements of \(n\mathrm{Pr}{-}2{-}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) were fully consistent with the exclusive formation of ammonium carbamate chains (Figure S18). Overall, our additional experiments on dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})\) indicate that interactions between chemisorbed species in the \(a b\) plane of dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) are an important feature of the mixed cooperative mechanism, lending additional support to the structure proposed in Figure 8.
The bulky methyl groups on the diaminopropane backbone of dmpn offer a means to alter the chemisorption mechanisms and the resulting thermodynamics for diamine– \(\cdot\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) materials. Notably, our results help to explain the previously measured15 larger entropic penalty associated with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) chemisorption in dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) compared to the penalty in materials that form only ammonium carbamate chains. Indeed, the interactions between ammonium carbamates and carbamic acids in the mixed structure presumably result in additional entropy penalties. The results obtained here also help to explain the isotherm (and isobar) shape observed for dmpn– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) , which features a less sharp adsorption step than typical diamine– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) materials.15 At pressures associated with the step region, a mixture of ammonium carbamate and carbamic acid forms and hinders the full cooperative adsorption process that would result upon exclusive ammonium carbamate chain formation. The mixed mechanism may also account for the hysteresis observed upon desorption (Figure 9a), corresponding to retention of approximately \(50\%\) of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) .15 We tentatively propose that the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules bound as carbamic acids in the mixed structure are more easily removed upon desorption than the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules bound within ammonium carbamate chains, and that the ammonium carbamates thus account for the desorption hysteresis. Additional adsorption and desorption experiments showed that this hysteresis is most pronounced as equilibrium conditions
The Role of Water in Directing Chemisorption. Given that water vapor is present in most practical gas mixtures of interest and that all of the measurements presented so far were performed under dry conditions, it is critical to evaluate the effect of water on the chemisorption mechanisms in diamine– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) materials. We first chose to study \(\mathrm{e}{-}2{-}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) , which exclusively forms ammonium carbamate chains as discussed above (Figure 10a). At room temperature, activated \(\mathrm{e}{-}2{-}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) was first exposed to wet \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) before dosing with dry \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) for an additional \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . Proton NMR data collected before and after dosing with wet \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) confirmed the adsorption of water in the material (Figure S20), and \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR data revealed a spectrum very similar to that obtained under dry conditions, with a single resonance at \(162.0~\mathrm{ppm}\) assigned to ammonium carbamate chains. Separate experiments in which \(\mathrm{e}{-}2{-}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) was first exposed to dry \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and then wet \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at natural isotopic abundance gave very similar results (Figure S21). These results suggest that ammonium carbamate chains can form and persist in the presence of water, which is essential for the practical application of these materials. Our results also help to explain previous studies of diamine– \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) materials, wherein
measurements under dry and humid conditions resulted in similar step-like adsorption profiles and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacities.16,39 Recent DFT calculations have suggested that ammonium carbamate chains are stabilized by the presence of water via hydrogen bonding interactions, indicating that water may have a beneficial effect on the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture performance of these materials.24
In contrast to \(\mathrm{e}{-}2{-}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) , \(\mathrm{dmpn-Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) undergoes a dramatic change in its chemisorption mechanism in the presence of water (Figure 10b). At room temperature, activated \(\mathrm{dmpn-Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) was exposed to wet \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) before dosing with \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) for \(15~\mathrm{h}\) . Under wet conditions, the major chemisorption products are assigned to ammonium carbamate chains on the basis of the observed \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) chemical shifts and a two dimensional HETCOR spectrum, which indicates at least two different ammonium carbamate conformations (Figure S22). This result deviates from that observed under dry conditions, wherein the mixed chemisorption structure dominates at the same gas pressures (Figure 10b). Despite the change of the chemisorption mechanism under wet conditions, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity is maintained in \(\mathrm{dmpn-Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) , as shown previ
ously via breakthrough experiments under dry and wet conditions.15 Our findings are fully consistent with the known instability of carbamic acid under wet conditions in other materials,35,40 as well as the stabilization of ammonium carbamates by hydrogen-bonding interactions with water.24
## CONCLUSIONS
We have carried out a systematic study of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) chemisorption in materials of the type diamine– \(\cdot\mathrm{M}_{2}\) (dobpdc). The canonical chemisorption products of ammonium carbamate chains and carbamic acid pairs have been thoroughly characterized using multinuclear NMR experiments and \(\mathrm{vdW}\) -corrected DFT calculations. Our results demonstrate the importance of using multiple experimental and calculated NMR parameters to make accurate structural assignments. In particular, \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR experiments and calculations alone may be insufficient to make accurate structural assignments, owing to the small range of \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) chemical shifts for the possible chemisorption products and relatively large discrepancies between experimental and calculated shift values. Nonetheless, the ranges determined here for chemical shift parameters of different chemisorption products can guide structural assignments following \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in the wider family of amine-functionalized materials (Figure 6). Ultimately, experiments and calculations that give information on the connectivity of the various groups (such as \(^{1}\mathrm{H}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) two dimensional HETCOR experiments) can enable clearer assignments of carbamic acid and ammonium carbamate groups.
## EXPERIMENTAL
Materials. Diamines and solvents were purchased from commercial sources and used as received. The ligand \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{dobpdc}\) was purchased from Hangzhou Trylead Chemical Technology Co and used as received.
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})\) . The framework was prepared according to the literature procedure.14 An Erlenmeyer flask was charged with \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) ( \(11.5\,\mathrm{g},\) \(45.0\;\mathrm{mmol}\) , 1.24 equiv), \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -dihydroxy-[1,1'-
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})\) . The framework was prepared according to the literature procedure.14 An Erlenmeyer flask was charged with \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) ( \(11.5\,\mathrm{g},\) \(45.0\;\mathrm{mmol}\) , 1.24 equiv), \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -dihydroxy-[1,1'-biphenyl]-3,3'-dicarboxylic acid ( \(9.90\,\mathrm{g},\) \(36.0\;\mathrm{mmol}\) , 1.00 equiv), N,N-dimethylformamide \(\left(90\;\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , and methanol ( \(110\;\mathrm{mL}\) ). The mixture was sonicated until the solids dissolved and was then filtered through filter paper into a \(350~\mathrm{mL}\) screw-cap high-pressure reaction vessel equipped with a stir bar. The reaction mixture was sparged with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , and then the reaction vessel was sealed and the reaction mixture stirred slowly at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(14~\mathrm{h}\) , resulting in precipitation of a white solid from solution. The mixture was filtered to collect the solid, and the solid was quickly transferred to a Pyrex jar filled with N,N-dimethylformamide (500 mL). This was placed in an oven heated to \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and allowed to stand for at least \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) . At this time, the mixture was filtered and the solid was returned to the jar with fresh N,N-dimethylformamide \(\left(500\,\mathrm{\mL}\right)\) and placed in an oven heated to \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for a further \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) . This washing process was repeated a total of three times. The N,N-dimethylformamide was replaced with methanol \(\left(500\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , and this washing process was repeated a further three times with methanol \(\left(24\,\mathrm{h}\right)\) . The remaining solid was activated under flowing \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) , transferred to a glass adsorption tube equipped with a Micromeritics TransSeal, and activated for an additional \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) under reduced pressure \(\left(<10\,\upmu\mathrm{bar}\right)\) at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Activated \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) was obtained as a white solid. Langmuir surface area determined from the \(77\,\mathrm{K}\,\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm: \(3900\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) .
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Zn}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})\) . An Erlenmeyer flask was charged with \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) ( \(3.72\,\mathrm{g},\) \(12.5\,\mathrm{mmol}\) , 2.30 equiv), \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -dihydroxy-[1,1'-biphenyl]-3,3'-dicarboxylic acid ( \(1.37\,\mathrm{g},\) \(5.00\;\mathrm{mmol}\) , 1.00 equiv), N,N-dimethylformamide \(\left(250\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , and methanol \(\left(250\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) . The mixture was sonicated until the solids dissolved and filtered through filter paper into a \(1\,\mathrm{L}\) Pyrex jar. The reaction mixture was vigorously sparged with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) , and the jar was placed in an oven at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and allowed to stand for \(14\,\mathrm{h}\) , resulting in precipitation of an off-white solid from solution. The non-homogenous mixture was filtered to collect the solid, and the solid was quickly transferred to a Pyrex jar filled with N,N-dimethylformamide \(\left(500\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) . The jar was placed in an oven heated to \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and allowed to stand for at least \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) . At this time, the mixture was filtered and the solid was returned to the jar with fresh N,N-dimethylformamide \(\left(500\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) and placed again in an oven heated to \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) . This washing process was repeated a total of three times. The N,N-dimethylformamide was replaced with methanol \(\left(500\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , and this washing process was repeated a further three times with methanol. The mixture was filtered, and the collected solid was transferred to a Schlenk flask and activated under flowing \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{8\,\mathrm{h}}\) followed by heating under high vacuum at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) . The Schlenk flask was transferred into a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -filled glovebox, and the solid was transferred to a glass adsorption tube equipped with a Micromeritics TransSeal and activated for an additional \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) under high vacuum \(\left(<10\,\upmu\mathrm{bar}\right)\) at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Activated \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) was obtained as \(1.89\,\mathrm{g}\) \((94\%)\) of an off-white solid. Langmuir surface area determined from the \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\,\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm: \(3130\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) . Note: methanol-solvated \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) slowly degrades in the presence of air and thus should be stored activated in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -filled glovebox when not in use.
tertiary carbon – left hand resonance), and \(33.4\,\mathrm{ppm}\) (glycine),56 respectively. NMR experiments were performed at room temperature without temperature control. The temperature increase due to frictional heating at \(15\,\mathrm{kHz}\) MAS in a \(3.2\,\mathrm{mm}\) is expected to be approximately \(12\,\mathrm{K}\).57
Computational Details. In order to elucidate the local structures of the various diamine-appended frameworks, we performed first-principles density functional theory (DFT) calculations. We used a plane-wave basis and projector augmented-wave (PAW)58,59 pseudopotentials with the Vienna ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) code.60–63 To include the effect of the van der Waals (vdW) dispersive interactions on binding energies, we performed structural relaxations with vdW dispersion-corrected functionals (vdW-DF2)29 as implemented in VASP. DFT binding energies were calculated using \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}=E_{\mathrm{(CO2)}}\) -diamine–M2(dobpdc) \(\left(E_{\mathrm{(diamine-M2(dobpdc)+E_{CO2})}}\right)\), where the contributions of the zero-point energy and thermal energy corrections are neglected, as these are generally less than \(2\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\).24 For these CO2 binding energy calculations, we used (i) a 1 point sampling of the Brillouin zone and (ii) a \(600\,\mathrm{eV}\) plane-wave cutoff energy. We explicitly treated two valence electrons for \(\mathrm{Mg}\left(3\mathrm{s}^{2}\right)\), twelve for \(\mathrm{Zn}\left(3d^{10}4\mathrm{s}^{2}\right)\), six for \(\mathrm{C}\left(2\mathrm{s}^{2}2\mathrm{p}^{2}\right)\), five for \(\mathrm{N}\left(2\mathrm{s}^{2}2\mathrm{p}^{2}\right)\), four for \(\mathrm{C}\left(2\mathrm{s}^{2}2\mathrm{p}^{2}\right)\), and one for \(\mathrm{H}\left(1\mathrm{s}^{2}\right)\). All structural relaxations were performed with a Gaussian smearing of the electronic occupations of \(0.05\,\mathrm{eV}\).65 The ions were relaxed until the Hellmann-Feynman forces were less than \(0.02\,\mathrm{eV}\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\). The convergence threshold for self-consistency in the total energy was \(10^{-5}\,\mathrm{eV}\).
For NMR chemical shift calculations, we performed structural relaxations with (i) a \(1000\,\mathrm{eV}\) plane-wave cutoff energy, (ii) a \(0.01\,\mathrm{eV}\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) force criterion, (iii) a \(1\times1\times3\) p-point grid, (iv) a \(10^{-7}\,\mathrm{eV}\) self-consistency criterion, and (v) the PBE functional66 with the D3 vdW correction;67,68 see Table S4 for optimized lattice parameters. In these input criteria, the isotropic chemical shielding \(\left(\sigma_{\mathrm{iso}}\right)\) converged within \(0.1\,\mathrm{ppm}\). The isotropic chemical shift \(\left(\delta_{\mathrm{iso}}\right)\) was obtained using \(\delta_{\mathrm{iso}}=-(\sigma_{\mathrm{iso}}-\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}})\), where \(\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}\) is a reference shielding value that is \(30.9\,\mathrm{ppm}\), \(170.5\,\mathrm{ppm}\), and \(225.0\,\mathrm{ppm}\) for \(\mathrm{^{\prime}H}\), \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) and \(^{15}\mathrm{N}\), respectively. The \(\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}\) values for \(\mathrm{^{\prime}H}\) and \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) were obtained by first computing \(\sigma_{\mathrm{iso}}\) values for cocaine (CSD entry code: COCANN10 was used as the starting point and the structure was geometry optimized before NMR calculation). Cocaine was used for shift referencing as it has several \(\mathrm{^{\prime}H}\) and \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) resonances for comparison between experiment and calculation. DFT-calculated values were compared to published experimental values,69 with \(\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}\) given as the y-intercept of a linear fit (with a fixed gradient of \(-1\)) in a plot of \(\delta_{\mathrm{iso}}\) (experimental) vs. \(\sigma_{\mathrm{iso}}\) (calculated). The \(\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}\) value for \(\mathrm{^{\prime}N}\) was determined by comparison of DFT-calculated \(\sigma_{\mathrm{iso}}\) and the experimental \(\delta_{\mathrm{iso}}\) value for glycine.
Gas Adsorption. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm for e-2– \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) was obtained by a volumetric method using a Micromeritics 3Flex gas adsorption analyzer and research-grade \((>99.998\%)\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and He. Approximately \(80\,\mathrm{mg}\) of the framework was loaded into a preweighed sample tube, which was first transferred to a Micromeritics 2420 degas manifold and heated to \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) under reduced pressure \((<10\,\upmu\mathrm{bar})\). The sample was held under vacuum at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(\sim3\,\mathrm{h}\), after which the evacuated tube was weighed to determine the mass of the degassed sample. The sample was then transferred air-free to the analysis port of the 3Flex adsorption analyzer and immersed in a temperature-controlled oil bath at \(26(1)\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Equilibration was defined as \(<0.01\%\) change in average pressure over an equilibration interval of \(50\,\mathrm{s}\). Gas adsorption isotherm data for dmpn– \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) and dmnp– \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was taken directly from a previous study.15
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) experiments were conducted using a TA Instruments TGA QS000. Isobars were measured using a temperature ramp rate of \(1\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\), except where otherwise indicated. Samples were first activated at \(130\) or \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (for dmpn– \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\), respectively) under flowing \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) prior to switching the gas stream to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and cooling. Masses are uncorrected for buoyancy effects.
